{"comparisons":[],"failures":[],"frontier":[],"initial":{"controlled-language":{"coverage":[{"reason":"Paragraph 10 supports competing accountability and substantive-performance readings; outsourcing does not remove responsibility.","status":"INTERPRETED","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u0.0"},{"reason":"Footnote 1 reinforces full responsibility and conditionally refers to external factors; it supplies no complete performance standard.","status":"INTERPRETED","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u1.224"},{"reason":"Proper ownership records and operational compatibility are express substantive requirements, relevant to performance and footnote 5's minimum confirmation.","status":"INTERPRETED","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u2.729"},{"reason":"Paragraph 14 supplies confirmation, a request-qualified demonstration, their objects and the SFC satisfaction standard.","status":"INTERPRETED","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u3.944"},{"reason":"Footnote 5 supplies minimum content and confidence language, but its unqualified demonstration wording creates an attachment ambiguity.","status":"INTERPRETED","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u4.1185"},{"reason":"Paragraph 15 separately makes obtaining third-party audit or verification conditional on an SFC request.","status":"INTERPRETED","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u5.1464"},{"reason":"Paragraph 16 separately makes obtaining a satisfactory legal opinion supporting the application conditional on an SFC request.","status":"INTERPRETED","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u6.1694"},{"reason":"The register-maintenance allocation illustrates delegation of performance without displacing the Product Provider's retained responsibility; duplicated in the next unit.","status":"INTERPRETED","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u7.1802"},{"reason":"Adds a distinct request-triggered duty to advise the Commission of register addresses; it does not qualify register maintenance itself.","status":"INTERPRETED","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u8.1941"},{"reason":"Valuation-specific care, monitoring, supervision and review provide contextual distinctions, not an automatically applicable tokenisation standard.","status":"CONTEXT","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u9.2169"},{"reason":"Intermediary issuance responsibility corroborates the outsourcing distinction within its own expressly limited actor and activity scope.","status":"CONTEXT","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u10.2702"},{"reason":"Intermediary due diligence and pre-activity satisfaction with controls are separate contextual duties; the referenced appendices are absent.","status":"CONTEXT","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u11.3045"}],"dimensions":[{"dimension":"scope","explanation":"The selected duties address Product Providers and their adopted tokenisation arrangements. Register keepers, valuation management companies and intermediaries have distinct actor and activity conditions. Those provisions cannot be transferred wholesale to every Product Provider.","source_unit_ids":["dc689bdf0ffcf.u0.0","dc689bdf0ffcf.u1.224","dc689bdf0ffcf.u7.1802","dc689bdf0ffcf.u9.2169","dc689bdf0ffcf.u10.2702","dc689bdf0ffcf.u11.3045"],"status":"PROPOSED"},{"dimension":"definitions","explanation":"Ultimate responsibility, operational soundness, proper records, integrity, satisfactory opinion and high confidence lack complete assessment criteria here. Allocation of responsibility, supervisory conduct and operational outcomes are different propositions; none automatically proves the others.","source_unit_ids":["dc689bdf0ffcf.u0.0","dc689bdf0ffcf.u2.729","dc689bdf0ffcf.u3.944","dc689bdf0ffcf.u4.1185","dc689bdf0ffcf.u6.1694"],"status":"AMBIGUOUS"},{"dimension":"exceptions","explanation":"Outsourcing is expressly ineffective as an escape from responsibility. The applicability qualification in footnote 1 attaches to referenced factors, not to retained responsibility. The SFC request condition qualifies demonstration in paragraph 14 and obtaining assistance in paragraphs 15–16, subject to the footnote 5 ambiguity.","source_unit_ids":["dc689bdf0ffcf.u0.0","dc689bdf0ffcf.u1.224","dc689bdf0ffcf.u3.944","dc689bdf0ffcf.u4.1185","dc689bdf0ffcf.u5.1464","dc689bdf0ffcf.u6.1694"],"status":"AMBIGUOUS"},{"dimension":"modality","explanation":"The circular uses should; register provisions use must, and valuation provisions distinguish shall from should. These excerpts do not establish their legal enforceability or consequences. Paragraph 14's confirmation is unqualified by a request on its ordinary syntax; whether footnote 5 independently requires demonstration remains disputed.","source_unit_ids":["dc689bdf0ffcf.u0.0","dc689bdf0ffcf.u3.944","dc689bdf0ffcf.u4.1185","dc689bdf0ffcf.u7.1802","dc689bdf0ffcf.u9.2169"],"status":"AMBIGUOUS"},{"dimension":"time","explanation":"Remaining responsible and maintaining records indicate continuing responsibilities. Requests activate specified steps without supplied deadlines. Valuation monitoring is ongoing and review periodic; intermediary satisfaction precedes related activities. No effective date, confirmation cadence or assessment period is supplied.","source_unit_ids":["dc689bdf0ffcf.u0.0","dc689bdf0ffcf.u2.729","dc689bdf0ffcf.u3.944","dc689bdf0ffcf.u5.1464","dc689bdf0ffcf.u6.1694","dc689bdf0ffcf.u9.2169","dc689bdf0ffcf.u11.3045"],"status":"PROPOSED"},{"dimension":"dependencies","explanation":"Footnote 5 incorporates paragraphs 10–13, whose complete content and mapping are unavailable. Footnote 1 references applicable Appendix Part A factors; the intermediary provisions reference Parts A and B. Footnote 2 is absent. These gaps and missing actual facts prevent a complete fulfillment conclusion.","source_unit_ids":["dc689bdf0ffcf.u1.224","dc689bdf0ffcf.u2.729","dc689bdf0ffcf.u4.1185","dc689bdf0ffcf.u11.3045"],"status":"AMBIGUOUS"}],"questions":["Does paragraph 10 impose retained accountability alone, or a substantive performance obligation as well, and what applicable standard distinguishes adequate supervision from an outcome guarantee?","What evidence establishes the Product Provider's actual authority, access to records, oversight, intervention and remediation, and the arrangement's operational and ownership-record outcomes over the relevant period?","What are the complete paragraphs 10–13, footnote 2 and applicable Appendix factors, and which actor-specific provisions apply to this Product Provider?","Does footnote 5 elaborate the content of paragraph 14's request-qualified demonstration, or impose a minimum demonstration independently of a request?","Does high confidence qualify only the smart-contract assurance, or also compliance with paragraphs 10–13? What evidence and assessment method satisfy that standard and the SFC?","When and how must confirmation occur, and what response period applies to each request?","Has the SFC made a relevant request under paragraph 14, 15 or 16, what did it require, and what confirmation, demonstration, audit or verification, or legal opinion was actually supplied?"],"readings":[{"assumptions":["Responsibility can remain with the Product Provider while another actor performs register maintenance or technical operations.","Valuation supervision requirements are contextual comparisons unless independently applicable."],"citations":[{"quote":"Product Providers should remain and be ultimately responsible for the management and operational soundness of the tokenisation arrangement adopted and record keeping of ownership, regardless of any outsourcing arrangement1.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u0.0"},{"quote":"1 Given that these tokenised products are offered to the public in Hong Kong and the importance of maintaining proper records to reflect investors’ ownership, the Product Provider should remain fully responsible, despite any outsourcing arrangement such as those envisaged under 6.8 of the Code on Unit Trusts and Mutual Funds. For factors of consideration, please also refer to Part A of the Appendix to the Tokenised Securities Circular, where applicable, when formulating the tokenisation arrangement.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u1.224"},{"quote":"6.8 The scheme, or in the case of a unit trust, the trustee or the person so appointed by the trustee must maintain a register of holders. The Commission must be advised on request of the address(es) where the register is kept.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u8.1941"},{"quote":"6.11C Where a third party is engaged in the valuation of a scheme, the management company shall exercise reasonable care, skill and diligence in the selection, appointment and ongoing monitoring of such third party in ensuring such entity possesses the appropriate level of knowledge, experience and resources that commensurate with the appropriate valuation policies and procedures for each scheme. The valuation activities of such third party should be subject to ongoing supervision and periodic review by the management company.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u9.2169"},{"quote":"13. Where intermediaries issue (eg, fund managers of tokenised funds) or are substantially involved in the issuance of the Tokenised Securities which they intend to deal in or advise on, they remain responsible for the overall operation of the tokenisation arrangement notwithstanding any outsourcing to third-party vendors/service providers.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u10.2702"},{"quote":"17. Where intermediaries deal in, advise on, or manage portfolios investing in Tokenised Securities, intermediaries are reminded to conduct due diligence on the issuers and their third-party vendors/service providers involved in the tokenisation arrangement as well as the features and risks arising from the tokenisation arrangement. 18. Intermediaries should understand and be satisfied with the controls implemented by the issuers and their third-party vendors/service providers to manage ownership and technology risks of the Tokenised Securities before they engage in related activities. Please refer to Parts A and B of the Appendix to this circular for a non-exhaustive list of factors for consideration.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u11.3045"}],"distinction":"This reading treats paragraph 10 principally as allocation of continuing accountability. An operational failure does not itself show that responsibility was transferred, although it may breach another requirement. Formal allocation alone does not establish adequate exercise of responsibility.","family":"scope","formalization":null,"local_id":"responsibility_allocation","questions":["What substantive supervisory conduct, beyond retaining accountability, does the applicable standard require?","Can the Product Provider actually obtain information and direct correction despite outsourcing?"],"statement":"Paragraph 10 and footnote 1 principally require the Product Provider to retain full ultimate accountability for management, operational soundness and ownership records despite outsourcing. Contracts, governance arrangements and actual ability to oversee and intervene could support retained responsibility; evidence of supervision would support its exercise. Neither nominal authority nor vendor appointment establishes sound operations or proper records. Fulfillment beyond the allocation question remains unresolved without an applicable standard and facts.","subject":"Paragraph 10 and footnote 1: retained accountability"},{"assumptions":["Responsibility for soundness and record keeping carries a substantive performance component when read with the express requirement to ensure proper records and compatibility.","The text does not establish strict liability for every incident or an absolute guarantee against failure."],"citations":[{"quote":"Product Providers should remain and be ultimately responsible for the management and operational soundness of the tokenisation arrangement adopted and record keeping of ownership, regardless of any outsourcing arrangement1.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u0.0"},{"quote":"1 Given that these tokenised products are offered to the public in Hong Kong and the importance of maintaining proper records to reflect investors’ ownership, the Product Provider should remain fully responsible, despite any outsourcing arrangement such as those envisaged under 6.8 of the Code on Unit Trusts and Mutual Funds. For factors of consideration, please also refer to Part A of the Appendix to the Tokenised Securities Circular, where applicable, when formulating the tokenisation arrangement.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u1.224"},{"quote":"Product Providers should ensure that proper records of token holders’ ownership interests in the product are maintained and the tokenisation arrangement is operationally compatible with service providers involved2.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u2.729"},{"quote":"5 Product Providers should confirm and demonstrate to the SFC that, at a minimum, they comply with the requirements as set out in paragraphs 10 to 13 above and the smart contracts are not subject to any contract vulnerabilities or security flaws with a high level of confidence.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u4.1185"}],"distinction":"Unlike the allocation-focused reading, this reading requires evidence of satisfactory substantive performance to conclude that paragraph 10 itself is fulfilled. Authority and diligent supervision are relevant but cannot substitute for the required operational and record-keeping condition.","family":"modality","formalization":null,"local_id":"responsibility_performance","questions":["What operational and record-keeping condition counts as satisfactory, and how do incidents, duration and remediation affect that assessment?","Is the governing standard outcome-based, conduct-based, or a combination?"],"statement":"Read substantively with the requirement to ensure proper ownership records and operational compatibility, paragraph 10 requires retained accountability exercised so that management, operations and record keeping meet the applicable standard. A fulfillment conclusion would require evidence of retained authority, effective supervisory conduct and satisfactory operational and record-keeping performance. The excerpts do not supply a complete standard or facts permitting that conclusion.","subject":"Paragraph 10 and footnote 1: substantive fulfillment"},{"assumptions":["Footnote 5 specifies paragraph 14's minimum content while preserving its request qualification for demonstration.","The narrow predicate below addresses only whether demonstration is called for; it does not assess confirmation, performance or SFC satisfaction."],"citations":[{"quote":"Product Providers should confirm and, upon SFC’s request, demonstrate to the SFC’s satisfaction the management and operational soundness of the tokenisation arrangement, record keeping of ownership and the integrity of the smart contracts5.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u3.944"},{"quote":"5 Product Providers should confirm and demonstrate to the SFC that, at a minimum, they comply with the requirements as set out in paragraphs 10 to 13 above and the smart contracts are not subject to any contract vulnerabilities or security flaws with a high level of confidence.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u4.1185"},{"quote":"Product Providers should, upon SFC’s request, obtain third party audit or verification on the management and operational soundness of the tokenisation arrangement, record keeping of ownership and integrity of the smart contracts.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u5.1464"},{"quote":"Product Providers should, upon SFC’s request, obtain satisfactory legal opinion to support its application.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u6.1694"}],"distinction":"Without a request, confirmation remains called for but demonstration under paragraph 14 is not activated. This differs from reading footnote 5 as an independent minimum demonstration duty. Absence of a request proves neither fulfillment nor SFC acceptance.","family":"exceptions","formalization":{"facts":[{"meaning":"The assessed actor and tokenisation arrangement fall within the Product Provider duties in paragraph 14 and footnote 5.","name":"provider_in_scope","requires_judgment":true,"source_unit_ids":["dc689bdf0ffcf.u3.944","dc689bdf0ffcf.u4.1185"],"type":"bool","unit":"Boolean"},{"meaning":"The SFC has made a request applicable to the assessed Product Provider and arrangement for the paragraph 14 demonstration.","name":"demonstration_requested","requires_judgment":true,"source_unit_ids":["dc689bdf0ffcf.u3.944"],"type":"bool","unit":"Boolean"}],"result":"demonstration_requested","result_type":"bool","scope":"provider_in_scope"},"local_id":"demonstration_request_qualified","questions":["Does footnote 5 preserve the main paragraph's request qualification?","Does high confidence attach only to freedom from smart-contract vulnerabilities and security flaws, as its placement suggests, or to both minimum propositions?"],"statement":"[TRUE_IS_SATISFIED] For an in-scope Product Provider, true means the request condition activating paragraph 14 demonstration is satisfied; false means that demonstration has not been activated by a request. Confirmation remains unqualified by a request. Confirmation and demonstration concern management and operational soundness, ownership record keeping and smart-contract integrity. Footnote 5 supplies minimum content: compliance with paragraphs 10–13 and high-confidence assurance that smart contracts have no contract vulnerabilities or security flaws. Paragraph 14 expressly subjects demonstration to SFC satisfaction. Paragraph 15 separately calls for obtaining third-party audit or verification on request; paragraph 16 separately calls for a satisfactory legal opinion supporting the application on request. Neither automatically follows merely from a paragraph 14 request.","subject":"Paragraph 14 demonstration trigger with footnote 5 as elaboration"},{"assumptions":["Footnote 5's unqualified direction to confirm and demonstrate creates a minimum demonstration duty independently of a request.","Paragraph 14's request qualification continues to govern its broader or specifically requested demonstration; this reconciliation is uncertain."],"citations":[{"quote":"Product Providers should confirm and, upon SFC’s request, demonstrate to the SFC’s satisfaction the management and operational soundness of the tokenisation arrangement, record keeping of ownership and the integrity of the smart contracts5.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u3.944"},{"quote":"5 Product Providers should confirm and demonstrate to the SFC that, at a minimum, they comply with the requirements as set out in paragraphs 10 to 13 above and the smart contracts are not subject to any contract vulnerabilities or security flaws with a high level of confidence.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u4.1185"},{"quote":"Product Providers should, upon SFC’s request, obtain third party audit or verification on the management and operational soundness of the tokenisation arrangement, record keeping of ownership and integrity of the smart contracts.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u5.1464"},{"quote":"Product Providers should, upon SFC’s request, obtain satisfactory legal opinion to support its application.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u6.1694"}],"distinction":"On this rival reading, no request is needed for the footnote's minimum demonstration. Its strongest weakness is that it reduces the apparent effect of paragraph 14's express request qualification. Footnote 5 does not similarly remove the independent request conditions in paragraphs 15 and 16.","family":"dependencies","formalization":{"facts":[{"meaning":"The assessed actor and tokenisation arrangement fall within the Product Provider duties in paragraph 14 and footnote 5.","name":"provider_in_scope","requires_judgment":true,"source_unit_ids":["dc689bdf0ffcf.u3.944","dc689bdf0ffcf.u4.1185"],"type":"bool","unit":"Boolean"},{"meaning":"The SFC has made a request applicable to the assessed Product Provider and arrangement for the paragraph 14 demonstration.","name":"demonstration_requested","requires_judgment":true,"source_unit_ids":["dc689bdf0ffcf.u3.944"],"type":"bool","unit":"Boolean"}],"result":"true","result_type":"bool","scope":"provider_in_scope"},"local_id":"demonstration_minimum_independent","questions":["Can the unqualified footnote impose a standing minimum demonstration while the main paragraph makes demonstration request-dependent?","Does paragraph 14's SFC satisfaction standard govern this standing minimum, and when must it be provided?"],"statement":"[TRUE_IS_SATISFIED] For an in-scope Product Provider, true means a minimum demonstration is called for under paragraph 14 and footnote 5 regardless of whether a request exists; false does not arise within this reading's scope. This is an obligation-activation predicate, not a finding of fulfillment. Footnote 5 independently requires confirmation and demonstration to the SFC of compliance with paragraphs 10–13 and the specified smart-contract assurance. Read together with paragraph 14, SFC satisfaction plausibly governs the demonstration, while the reach of high confidence remains unresolved. Without a request, this minimum duty persists; paragraphs 15 and 16 do not activate their respective requirements to obtain third-party audit or verification and a satisfactory legal opinion.","subject":"Paragraph 14 demonstration trigger with an independent footnote 5 minimum"}]},"normative":{"coverage":[{"reason":"Interpreted through competing responsibility readings: continuing allocation of responsibility and substantive performance.","status":"INTERPRETED","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u0.0"},{"reason":"Interpreted as preserving full responsibility despite outsourcing; the reference to applicable Appendix factors remains an unresolved dependency.","status":"INTERPRETED","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u1.224"},{"reason":"Interpreted as an express requirement concerning proper ownership records and operational compatibility, relevant to substantive fulfillment.","status":"INTERPRETED","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u2.729"},{"reason":"Interpreted through competing readings of the request condition and its relationship to footnote 5.","status":"INTERPRETED","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u3.944"},{"reason":"Interpreted as specifying minimum assurance content, with uncertainty about whether it independently requires demonstration and about confidence-standard attachment.","status":"INTERPRETED","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u4.1185"},{"reason":"Interpreted as request-conditioned procurement of third-party audit or verification, distinct from the provider's own confirmation and demonstration.","status":"INTERPRETED","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u5.1464"},{"reason":"Interpreted as request-conditioned procurement of a satisfactory legal opinion supporting the application.","status":"INTERPRETED","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u6.1694"},{"reason":"Interpreted with the fuller duplicate in unit 8: register maintenance may be assigned to specified actors without displacing the Product Provider's retained responsibility.","status":"INTERPRETED","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u7.1802"},{"reason":"Interpreted as a distinct mandatory register-maintenance rule and request-conditioned disclosure of register location; its actors are not automatically identical to the Product Provider.","status":"INTERPRETED","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u8.1941"},{"reason":"Contextual comparison supplies an express valuation-specific selection and supervision standard; it cannot automatically supply the missing tokenisation standard.","status":"CONTEXT","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u9.2169"},{"reason":"Contextual parallel preserves issuer-intermediary responsibility despite outsourcing, subject to its own issuance and intended dealing or advisory conditions.","status":"CONTEXT","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u10.2702"},{"reason":"Contextual intermediary duties concern due diligence and satisfaction with controls before related activities; they do not establish Product Provider fulfillment.","status":"CONTEXT","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u11.3045"}],"dimensions":[{"dimension":"scope","explanation":"The central provisions address Product Providers. Register custodians, valuation management companies, issuer-intermediaries and dealing, advising or portfolio-managing intermediaries have separately stated roles and triggers. Role overlap requires facts; it cannot be assumed.","source_unit_ids":["dc689bdf0ffcf.u0.0","dc689bdf0ffcf.u7.1802","dc689bdf0ffcf.u8.1941","dc689bdf0ffcf.u9.2169","dc689bdf0ffcf.u10.2702","dc689bdf0ffcf.u11.3045"],"status":"PROPOSED"},{"dimension":"definitions","explanation":"Ultimate or full responsibility, management and operational soundness, proper records, integrity, satisfactory legal opinion and high confidence lack operational definitions here. Retained accountability, supervisory conduct and achieved outcomes are distinguishable, but the text supplies no complete sufficiency test connecting them.","source_unit_ids":["dc689bdf0ffcf.u0.0","dc689bdf0ffcf.u1.224","dc689bdf0ffcf.u2.729","dc689bdf0ffcf.u3.944","dc689bdf0ffcf.u4.1185","dc689bdf0ffcf.u6.1694"],"status":"AMBIGUOUS"},{"dimension":"exceptions","explanation":"Outsourcing is expressly denied exculpatory effect. The applicability qualification concerns the Appendix factors, not continued responsibility. The SFC request is a condition on specified assurance actions, not a general exception to operational duties. Whether footnote 5 preserves that condition for demonstration is contested.","source_unit_ids":["dc689bdf0ffcf.u0.0","dc689bdf0ffcf.u1.224","dc689bdf0ffcf.u3.944","dc689bdf0ffcf.u4.1185","dc689bdf0ffcf.u5.1464","dc689bdf0ffcf.u6.1694","dc689bdf0ffcf.u10.2702"],"status":"AMBIGUOUS"},{"dimension":"modality","explanation":"The provider provisions use should; register maintenance and requested location disclosure use must; valuation selection and monitoring use shall while supervision and review use should. These differences are preserved without deciding enforceability. An assurance statement, substantive performance, third-party verification and legal opinion are distinct actions.","source_unit_ids":["dc689bdf0ffcf.u0.0","dc689bdf0ffcf.u2.729","dc689bdf0ffcf.u3.944","dc689bdf0ffcf.u4.1185","dc689bdf0ffcf.u5.1464","dc689bdf0ffcf.u6.1694","dc689bdf0ffcf.u8.1941","dc689bdf0ffcf.u9.2169"],"status":"PROPOSED"},{"dimension":"time","explanation":"No commencement date, response deadline or confirmation frequency is supplied. Remain indicates continuing responsibility; requests activate specified actions. Valuation supervision is ongoing and review periodic. Intermediary satisfaction with controls precedes related activities.","source_unit_ids":["dc689bdf0ffcf.u0.0","dc689bdf0ffcf.u3.944","dc689bdf0ffcf.u5.1464","dc689bdf0ffcf.u6.1694","dc689bdf0ffcf.u8.1941","dc689bdf0ffcf.u9.2169","dc689bdf0ffcf.u11.3045"],"status":"AMBIGUOUS"},{"dimension":"dependencies","explanation":"Footnote 1 depends on applicable Appendix Part A factors and references 6.8. Footnote 5 depends on the complete requirements in paragraphs 10–13, which are not all supplied; the paragraph numbered 13 in unit 10 belongs to the separate intermediary context and cannot fill that gap. Footnote 2 and Appendix Parts A and B are absent. These gaps prevent a complete fulfillment assessment.","source_unit_ids":["dc689bdf0ffcf.u1.224","dc689bdf0ffcf.u2.729","dc689bdf0ffcf.u4.1185","dc689bdf0ffcf.u7.1802","dc689bdf0ffcf.u8.1941","dc689bdf0ffcf.u10.2702","dc689bdf0ffcf.u11.3045"],"status":"AMBIGUOUS"}],"questions":["Which applicable standard determines sufficient management, operational soundness, ownership record keeping and smart-contract integrity, and how does it distinguish reasonable supervisory conduct from required outcomes?","What actual governance, outsourcing, intervention, monitoring, incident and ownership-reconciliation evidence shows both retained responsibility and performance?","What do the complete Product Provider paragraphs 10–13, footnote 2 and applicable Appendix provisions require?","Does footnote 5 specify the content of paragraph 14's request-conditioned demonstration, or independently require demonstration without a request?","Does high confidence qualify only the absence of smart-contract vulnerabilities and security flaws, or the full minimum assurance proposition? Does SFC satisfaction govern demonstration alone or confirmation as well?","What requests, if any, has the SFC made for demonstration, third-party audit or verification, legal opinion or register-location information, and with what scope and timing?","Which actor roles overlap in the actual arrangement, and what governing version, effective date and confirmation or response timing applies?"],"readings":[{"assumptions":["The selected slice identifies unit 0 as paragraph 10 and units 3, 5 and 6 as paragraphs 14, 15 and 16.","Continuing responsibility can coexist with delegation of operational tasks."],"citations":[{"quote":"Product Providers should remain and be ultimately responsible for the management and operational soundness of the tokenisation arrangement adopted and record keeping of ownership, regardless of any outsourcing arrangement1.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u0.0"},{"quote":"1 Given that these tokenised products are offered to the public in Hong Kong and the importance of maintaining proper records to reflect investors’ ownership, the Product Provider should remain fully responsible, despite any outsourcing arrangement such as those envisaged under 6.8 of the Code on Unit Trusts and Mutual Funds. For factors of consideration, please also refer to Part A of the Appendix to the Tokenised Securities Circular, where applicable, when formulating the tokenisation arrangement.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u1.224"},{"quote":"6.8 The scheme, or in the case of a unit trust, the trustee or the person so appointed by the trustee must maintain a register of holders.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u7.1802"},{"quote":"6.8 The scheme, or in the case of a unit trust, the trustee or the person so appointed by the trustee must maintain a register of holders. The Commission must be advised on request of the address(es) where the register is kept.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u8.1941"},{"quote":"6.11C Where a third party is engaged in the valuation of a scheme, the management company shall exercise reasonable care, skill and diligence in the selection, appointment and ongoing monitoring of such third party in ensuring such entity possesses the appropriate level of knowledge, experience and resources that commensurate with the appropriate valuation policies and procedures for each scheme. The valuation activities of such third party should be subject to ongoing supervision and periodic review by the management company.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u9.2169"},{"quote":"13. Where intermediaries issue (eg, fund managers of tokenised funds) or are substantially involved in the issuance of the Tokenised Securities which they intend to deal in or advise on, they remain responsible for the overall operation of the tokenisation arrangement notwithstanding any outsourcing to third-party vendors/service providers.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u10.2702"}],"distinction":"This reading treats paragraph 10 primarily as allocating non-transferable accountability. A service failure need not prove that responsibility was transferred, although it may prove a separate performance failure. It does not treat contractual allocation alone as fulfillment of all operational requirements.","family":"scope","formalization":null,"local_id":"responsibility_allocation","questions":["What authority and practical ability to direct, obtain information from and remediate the outsourced arrangement must accompany retained accountability?","Does the applicable standard make particular supervisory conduct sufficient for the paragraph 10 control despite an operational failure?"],"statement":"Paragraph 10 and footnote 1 can be read principally as retaining full and ultimate accountability with the Product Provider despite outsourcing. Governance and contractual allocation, backed by actual authority and conduct consistent with that allocation, could support a conclusion that responsibility was retained. They do not alone establish sound operations or proper ownership records. Section 6.8 permits specified actors to maintain the register and separately requires requested disclosure of its location; it does not displace the provider's responsibility. Section 6.11C expressly prescribes care in selection and monitoring for valuation outsourcing, but the packet does not make that standard the tokenisation fulfillment test.","subject":"Paragraph 10 and footnote 1: retained accountability as the selected control"},{"assumptions":["Paragraph 10 is read with the supplied ownership-record requirement and paragraph 14 assurance provisions.","Operational evidence is assessed against an applicable standard, rather than an invented guarantee against every failure."],"citations":[{"quote":"Product Providers should remain and be ultimately responsible for the management and operational soundness of the tokenisation arrangement adopted and record keeping of ownership, regardless of any outsourcing arrangement1.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u0.0"},{"quote":"1 Given that these tokenised products are offered to the public in Hong Kong and the importance of maintaining proper records to reflect investors’ ownership, the Product Provider should remain fully responsible, despite any outsourcing arrangement such as those envisaged under 6.8 of the Code on Unit Trusts and Mutual Funds. For factors of consideration, please also refer to Part A of the Appendix to the Tokenised Securities Circular, where applicable, when formulating the tokenisation arrangement.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u1.224"},{"quote":"Product Providers should ensure that proper records of token holders’ ownership interests in the product are maintained and the tokenisation arrangement is operationally compatible with service providers involved2.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u2.729"},{"quote":"Product Providers should confirm and, upon SFC’s request, demonstrate to the SFC’s satisfaction the management and operational soundness of the tokenisation arrangement, record keeping of ownership and the integrity of the smart contracts5.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u3.944"},{"quote":"5 Product Providers should confirm and demonstrate to the SFC that, at a minimum, they comply with the requirements as set out in paragraphs 10 to 13 above and the smart contracts are not subject to any contract vulnerabilities or security flaws with a high level of confidence.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u4.1185"},{"quote":"17. Where intermediaries deal in, advise on, or manage portfolios investing in Tokenised Securities, intermediaries are reminded to conduct due diligence on the issuers and their third-party vendors/service providers involved in the tokenisation arrangement as well as the features and risks arising from the tokenisation arrangement. 18. Intermediaries should understand and be satisfied with the controls implemented by the issuers and their third-party vendors/service providers to manage ownership and technology risks of the Tokenised Securities before they engage in related activities. Please refer to Parts A and B of the Appendix to this circular for a non-exhaustive list of factors for consideration.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u11.3045"}],"distinction":"This reading makes substantive performance part of fulfillment of the responsibility requirement itself. Under the allocation reading, retained responsibility can be established separately from failed performance; here, authority and diligent supervision alone cannot establish fulfillment where required outcomes are absent.","family":"modality","formalization":null,"local_id":"responsibility_performance","questions":["How does the applicable standard treat an incident despite diligent supervision, including materiality and remediation?","What evidence establishes operational compatibility, accurate ownership records and the other referenced requirements?"],"statement":"Read with the express requirement to ensure proper ownership records and operational compatibility, paragraph 10 can instead be understood as requiring the Product Provider to discharge substantive responsibility for the arrangement. A supported fulfillment conclusion would require evidence of retained accountability, effective supervisory conduct and operational and record-keeping performance meeting the applicable standard. Formal authority or an assertion of responsibility is insufficient; supervision is evidence of performance, not an automatic substitute for it. The intermediary due-diligence and prior satisfaction provisions concern separate actors and controls and cannot themselves establish provider fulfillment. The excerpt does not resolve whether every failure defeats fulfillment or whether an applicable standard permits a qualified assessment.","subject":"Paragraph 10 and footnote 1: substantive discharge of responsibility"},{"assumptions":["Footnote 5 elaborates paragraph 14's assurance content while preserving its express request condition for demonstration.","The formalization tests only the activation condition for paragraph 14 demonstration, not performance, confirmation, licence status or overall compliance."],"citations":[{"quote":"Product Providers should confirm and, upon SFC’s request, demonstrate to the SFC’s satisfaction the management and operational soundness of the tokenisation arrangement, record keeping of ownership and the integrity of the smart contracts5.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u3.944"},{"quote":"5 Product Providers should confirm and demonstrate to the SFC that, at a minimum, they comply with the requirements as set out in paragraphs 10 to 13 above and the smart contracts are not subject to any contract vulnerabilities or security flaws with a high level of confidence.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u4.1185"},{"quote":"Product Providers should, upon SFC’s request, obtain third party audit or verification on the management and operational soundness of the tokenisation arrangement, record keeping of ownership and integrity of the smart contracts.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u5.1464"},{"quote":"Product Providers should, upon SFC’s request, obtain satisfactory legal opinion to support its application.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u6.1694"}],"distinction":"This reading gives operative effect to the request phrase in paragraph 14 despite its omission from footnote 5. No request leaves confirmation and underlying duties in place but does not activate demonstration under the selected control.","family":"exceptions","formalization":{"facts":[{"meaning":"The assessed actor is a Product Provider within the supplied paragraph 14 provision.","name":"is_product_provider","requires_judgment":true,"source_unit_ids":["dc689bdf0ffcf.u3.944","dc689bdf0ffcf.u4.1185"],"type":"bool","unit":"Boolean"},{"meaning":"The SFC has made a request applicable to this provider for the demonstration described in paragraph 14; a request solely for another action does not automatically count.","name":"sfc_demonstration_requested","requires_judgment":true,"source_unit_ids":["dc689bdf0ffcf.u3.944"],"type":"bool","unit":"Boolean"}],"result":"sfc_demonstration_requested","result_type":"bool","scope":"is_product_provider"},"local_id":"demonstration_request_conditioned","questions":["Does the SFC-satisfaction standard attach only to demonstration, as its placement suggests?","Does high confidence attach only to the smart-contract proposition, as its placement suggests, or also to compliance with paragraphs 10–13?"],"statement":"[TRUE_IS_SATISFIED] Within the Product Provider scope, true means paragraph 14's request condition for demonstration is satisfied; false means that condition is not satisfied. Confirmation remains unconditioned by a request. Both actions concern management and operational soundness, ownership record keeping and smart-contract integrity; footnote 5 sets a minimum content of compliance with paragraphs 10–13 and absence of contract vulnerabilities or security flaws with high confidence. The strongest grammatical reading attaches SFC satisfaction to demonstration and high confidence to the smart-contract proposition, although broader attachment remains unresolved. With no request, demonstration is not activated on this reading; substantive duties and confirmation remain. Paragraph 15 separately conditions obtaining third-party audit or verification on a request and covers the same three subjects. Paragraph 16 separately conditions obtaining a satisfactory legal opinion supporting the application on a request. Neither provision establishes a routine audit or opinion requirement in the absence of its request.","subject":"Paragraph 14 demonstration trigger with footnote 5 supplying minimum content"},{"assumptions":["Footnote 5's unqualified direction to confirm and demonstrate has independent operative effect for its stated minimum content.","This reading retains paragraph 14's request condition for any broader demonstration and recognizes the resulting textual tension."],"citations":[{"quote":"Product Providers should confirm and, upon SFC’s request, demonstrate to the SFC’s satisfaction the management and operational soundness of the tokenisation arrangement, record keeping of ownership and the integrity of the smart contracts5.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u3.944"},{"quote":"5 Product Providers should confirm and demonstrate to the SFC that, at a minimum, they comply with the requirements as set out in paragraphs 10 to 13 above and the smart contracts are not subject to any contract vulnerabilities or security flaws with a high level of confidence.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u4.1185"},{"quote":"Product Providers should, upon SFC’s request, obtain third party audit or verification on the management and operational soundness of the tokenisation arrangement, record keeping of ownership and integrity of the smart contracts.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u5.1464"},{"quote":"Product Providers should, upon SFC’s request, obtain satisfactory legal opinion to support its application.","unit_id":"dc689bdf0ffcf.u6.1694"}],"distinction":"Unlike the request-conditioned reading, the omission of a request qualification in footnote 5 creates an independently expected minimum demonstration. This is textually possible but weaker because it reduces the work done by paragraph 14's express condition. It supplies no basis for removing the separate request conditions in paragraphs 15 and 16.","family":"dependencies","formalization":{"facts":[{"meaning":"The assessed actor is a Product Provider within the supplied paragraph 14 provision.","name":"is_product_provider","requires_judgment":true,"source_unit_ids":["dc689bdf0ffcf.u3.944","dc689bdf0ffcf.u4.1185"],"type":"bool","unit":"Boolean"},{"meaning":"The SFC has made a request applicable to this provider for the demonstration described in paragraph 14; a request solely for another action does not automatically count.","name":"sfc_demonstration_requested","requires_judgment":true,"source_unit_ids":["dc689bdf0ffcf.u3.944"],"type":"bool","unit":"Boolean"}],"result":"true","result_type":"bool","scope":"is_product_provider"},"local_id":"demonstration_minimum_independent","questions":["Is footnote 5 independently operative or solely explanatory of the main paragraph?","If independently operative, when and in what form must the minimum demonstration be supplied without a request?","Does SFC satisfaction extend to the independently required minimum demonstration?"],"statement":"[TRUE_IS_SATISFIED] Within the Product Provider scope, true means the condition for the minimum demonstration specified by footnote 5 is satisfied independently of an SFC request. The constant true expresses this proposed unconditional activation, not an invented fact or a finding that demonstration occurred; false does not arise within scope on this reading. Footnote 5 can be read as independently requiring both confirmation and demonstration to the SFC of compliance with paragraphs 10–13 and the high-confidence smart-contract proposition. With no request, that minimum demonstration would remain expected, while paragraph 14 still expressly conditions its broader demonstration on a request. The precise confidence and satisfaction standards remain unresolved. Paragraphs 15 and 16 still require their respective requests for third-party audit or verification and a satisfactory supporting legal opinion; footnote 5 does not expressly expand those provisions.","subject":"Footnote 5 as an independent minimum demonstration requirement"}]}},"java_checks":[],"live_provider":true,"model_calls":2,"no_progress":0,"nodes":[],"pending":null,"provider_id":"legal-gap.optional-tools.v1.compact.v2","retrievals":[],"rounds":0,"roundtrips":[],"run_id":"run.8adc12b432874c5bb1e8b4730a0ab93f","stop":null}